Verified independently. karr writes mojibake into its own refs, hands mojibake
to agents through `--json`, and cannot read a correctly encoded UTF-8 task file
at all. The interop goal ("task file format 100% compatible with kanban-md") is
broken for everything outside ASCII.

## Mechanism

`YAML::XS::Dump` returns UTF-8 **octets** and `Load` expects octets, but karr
mixes those with character-level IO and never decodes `@ARGV`:

- `Task.pm:132` — `Dump` output (octets) treated as characters
- `Task.pm:141`/`:146` — `Load` fed a character string
- `Task.pm:158` — `slurp_utf8` (decodes)
- `Task.pm:167` — `spew_utf8` (encodes again)

## Reproduction — writing

    karr create "Fix Ünicode — ärger"
    git cat-file -p <ref>:data | grep '^title:'
      -> title: "Fix Ã\x9Cnicode â\x80\x94 Ã¤rger"     # double-encoded in the ref

    karr show 1        -> Task #1: Fix Ünicode — ärger   # looks fine!
    karr show 1 --json -> {"title":"Fix Ãnicode â Ã¤rger"}

The plain-text output only looks correct because two encoding errors cancel out.
`--json` is the one an agent parses, and it is wrong.

## Reproduction — reading

A correctly UTF-8-encoded task file (title `Café latte`, `c3 a9` on disk):

    karr import --yes
    -> YAML::XS::Load Error: The problem:
           invalid trailing UTF-8 octet
       was found at document: 0
    exit 1

So karr cannot import any kanban-md file with a non-ASCII character in the
frontmatter.

## Fix

Decide one boundary and hold it: keep everything internal as character strings
and encode only at the IO edges (or the reverse), then make `Dump`/`Load`,
`slurp_utf8`/`spew_utf8`, the ref read/write path and `@ARGV` all agree. Decode
`@ARGV` explicitly (`Encode::decode` with the locale, or `use open`).

Ticket #33 fixed one symptom of this at the `karr skill show` print site; this
is the underlying inconsistency. Regression test needs the full round trip:
argv -> ref -> show -> --json -> materialize -> import -> ref, with a non-ASCII
title, body and tag, plus reading a file kanban-md wrote.
